ORIGINAL PLAN (October 2026)
Ramneek Kaur: Memory, Adaptive Difficulty and LSTM (FR-3, FR-5)
W1: Define the short-term and long-term memory structures and the mastery update formula. Write the spec for adaptive rules (when to promote, when to remediate).
W2: Implement mastery updates after each attempt and the memory read/write logic with Parth’s tables. Generate a simulated interaction dataset (response time, correctness, hints).
W3: Build the rule-based adaptive difficulty engine (increase after strong performance, remediate after repeated errors) and connect it to question selection.
W4: Train the LSTM on the Question → Response Time → Correctness → Hint Usage sequences. Run it asynchronously through the queue.
W5: Evaluate accuracy and reduction in repeated mistakes. Tune the thresholds, and write the testing report and the evaluation section of the project documentation.

FINAL WORK RECORD (project completed, November 2026)
Role: mastery model, adaptive difficulty and evaluation.

- Mastery model: Bayesian Knowledge Tracing per student per topic with
  textbook parameters (prior 0.30, guess 0.25, slip 0.10, learn 0.15), clamped
  to 0.01-0.99, with the learning step applied only after correct answers so a
  wrong answer always lowers mastery.
- Adaptive difficulty: the easy (below 0.40), medium and hard (above 0.75)
  thresholds that choose the next question's tier.
- Mastery display: the band first (Needs practice, Developing, Strong) and a
  percentage limited to 5-95% for reports, without changing stored values.
- Evaluation on simulated students: 5,000 students x 20 answers per scenario,
  BKT against three baselines (running accuracy, last answer, constant rate)
  using AUC, accuracy, Brier score and log loss, including a robustness
  scenario (tools/evaluate_mastery.py).
- Real-data evaluation design: the comparison on stored answers, the exclusion
  of demo and test accounts, the 200-answer / 10-student threshold and the
  bootstrap confidence interval (tools/evaluate_real_data.py).
- Tests for the mastery model, the display rules and the evaluation, and the
  evaluation section of the final report.

Changes from the plan: the LSTM was replaced by BKT, because an LSTM needs a
large real interaction dataset that did not exist, while BKT works from the
first answer and every number can be explained. Evaluation used simulated
students until enough real answers are collected.
